@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix dct: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .
@base <https://purl.org/archimate> .

### ArchiMate Profile Mechanism
<https://purl.org/archimate/profile> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://purl.org/archimate/owl> ;
    rdfs:label "ArchiMate Profile Mechanism" ;
    rdfs:comment "Defines the profileSpecialization annotation property used by the ArchiMate profile pattern. This property marks an OWL class as a domain-specific profile (specialization) of a standard ArchiMate element type. It is a class-level declaration, distinct from archimate:specialization which is an instance-level ArchiMate relationship per spec Section 5.4." ;
    dct:creator "Alberto D. Mendoza" ;
    dct:created "2026-07-05"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:license <http://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0> ;
    owl:versionInfo "Beta" .

# =============================================================================
# DESIGN NOTES — Instance Specialization vs Profile Specialization
# =============================================================================
#
# ArchiMate 3.2 defines specialization as an instance-level relationship
# (spec Section 5.4): "a specialization relationship is always allowed
# between two instances of the same element type." This is modeled as
# archimate:specialization (owl:ObjectProperty, owl:TransitiveProperty).
#
# Profiles are a different concept: they declare new OWL classes that
# refine standard ArchiMate element types with domain-specific semantics
# and typed attributes. This is a class-level (TBox) declaration, not an
# instance-level (ABox) relationship.
#
# archimate:profileSpecialization is an owl:AnnotationProperty that marks
# a class as a profile. It carries no OWL inference — rdfs:subClassOf
# handles type inference, and SPARQL property paths handle transitive
# querying:
#
#   # Find all profiles of DataObject, any depth
#   SELECT ?profile WHERE {
#       ?profile archimate:profileSpecialization+ archimate:DataObject .
#   }
#
# The three-declaration profile pattern:
#
#   ex:Person rdf:type owl:Class ;
#       rdfs:subClassOf archimate:BusinessActor ;          # OWL inference
#       archimate:profileSpecialization archimate:BusinessActor .  # profile marker
#
# Sub-profiles nest via rdfs:subClassOf, and still name the primitive
# directly:
#
#   ex:Employee rdf:type owl:Class ;
#       rdfs:subClassOf ex:Person, archimate:BusinessActor ; # OWL inference
#       archimate:profileSpecialization ex:Person .        # profile marker
#
# The primitive is repeated because discovery matches the asserted triple.
# Reaching archimate:BusinessActor through ex:Person is rdfs:subClassOf
# entailment, which a repository running a custom ruleset may not compute;
# there, a query of the form { ?c rdfs:subClassOf archimate:BusinessActor }
# never returns ex:Employee, and nothing reports the omission. The meta-shape
# avpat:ProfileNamesItsPrimitive enforces this.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fixed vs Open Attributes
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# An attribute is a schema-declared slot: an owl:DatatypeProperty (the term)
# plus a sh:property constraint in the profile shape (datatype, cardinality).
# Whether the slot carries a value at the profile level is orthogonal — it
# distinguishes two flavors of attribute, not attribute from property:
#
#   Open attribute   The shape constrains datatype and cardinality; each
#                    instance supplies its own value (e.g. a Person's
#                    firstName, or archimate:documentation at the core level).
#
#   Fixed attribute  The profile also pins the value with sh:hasValue
#                    (+ sh:maxCount 1). The profile supplies the value; an
#                    instance asserting a different value fails validation.
#                    Immutability here is conformance, not a lock — the only
#                    kind an open-world RDF graph offers.
#
# Neither flavor collapses into archimate:hasProperty (tier 1 of the property
# extension model, see ontology/archimate.ttl): a hasProperty pair has a value
# but no schema; an open attribute has schema but no value yet. The
# categorical split is declared-by-schema vs ad hoc — never valuedness.
#
# Inheritance: SHACL class targets select instances via rdfs:subClassOf*, so
# both flavors reach every sub-profile with no re-declaration. Shapes compose
# by conjunction, so a sub-profile can only narrow an inherited constraint
# (e.g. a parent's sh:in ("full-time" "part-time") pinned by the child to
# sh:hasValue "full-time") — it can never relax or override a fixed value.
# This matches the spec's specialization semantics: a specialization inherits
# everything, may add attributes, may restrict, and may never widen.
#
# A fixed attribute can be deployed two ways:
#
#   Constrain-only        Every instance must assert the pinned triple itself;
#                         validation rejects omission or deviation.
#
#   Constrain+materialize A standalone sh:SPARQLRule asserts the pinned value
#                         for every instance that lacks it, so instances stay
#                         lean and exports still carry the triple. Such rules
#                         load into any graph EXCEPT the SHACL shapes graph,
#                         same as the derivation rules (see
#                         derivation/archimate_derivation_rules.ttl for the
#                         placement rationale).
#
# Worked example: myModel:Employee — terms and materialization rule in
# ontology/archimate_profile_examples.ttl, shape in
# validation/archimate_validation_profile_examples.ttl.
#
# The pattern is enforced and regression-guarded:
# - validation/archimate_validation_profile_pattern.ttl — meta-shapes that
#   validate profile DEFINITIONS as data (three-declaration coherence, the
#   primitive named directly, fixed attribute well-formedness, no fixed value
#   contradicting an ancestor's, a single integer profileOrder, profileAspect
#   pointers landing on profile classes).
# - validation/conformance/ — frozen fixtures with expected-output oracles
#   for the instance-level guarantees (inheritance via subclass targeting,
#   fixed-value immutability, rule ordering); runner in
#   tools/profile_conformance.py.
#
# =============================================================================

<#profileSpecialization> rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "profile specialization" ;
    rdfs:comment "Marks an OWL class as a domain-specific profile (specialization) of a standard ArchiMate element type or another profile class. This is a class-level annotation for the profile pattern — it does not participate in OWL reasoning (rdfs:subClassOf handles inference) or ArchiMate derivation rules (archimate:specialization handles instance-level PDR1-4). Queryable via SPARQL property paths for profile discovery." .

# =============================================================================
# DESIGN NOTE — Sibling order
# =============================================================================
#
# A profile hierarchy is a tree of classes, and a tree has no order of its own.
# Where a profile encodes a framework that DOES state an order — a taxonomy read
# in a canonical sequence, a lifecycle whose stages run early to late — that
# order is part of what the profile asserts, not a rendering preference, and it
# belongs in the ontology beside the tree it orders.
#
# <#profileOrder> carries it. The value orders a class among the siblings that
# share its <#profileSpecialization> parent; it says nothing across parents, so
# two classes under different parents may hold the same value without conflict.
# A consumer walking the tree sorts each sibling set by it and falls back to the
# label where it is absent.
#
# Values are sparse — 10, 20, 30 — so a tenant adding a class between two
# existing ones needs no renumbering of what is already there. Gaps carry no
# meaning; only the relative order does.
#
# A profile either orders every one of its classes or none of them. A sibling
# set where some classes carry a value and others do not sorts by two different
# keys at once, which is more confusing than plain alphabetical order.
#
# =============================================================================

<#profileOrder> rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "profile order" ;
    rdfs:comment "The position of a profile class among the sibling classes that declare the same <#profileSpecialization> parent, lowest first. A class-level annotation for the profile pattern: it records an order the profiled framework itself states, and takes no part in OWL reasoning or the derivation rules. Sparse integers (10, 20, 30) leave room to insert between; the gaps carry no meaning, and values are comparable only within one sibling set." ;
    rdfs:range xsd:integer .

# =============================================================================
# DESIGN NOTE — Inherited context outside the subclass chain
# =============================================================================
#
# A profile class may carry class-level annotations that describe every
# instance of it — a panel of context a consumer shows beside an element,
# assembled by walking rdf:type/rdfs:subClassOf* and collecting what each
# ancestor states. Sometimes a profile has a second axis of such context that
# cannot be a parent without turning the tree into a lattice, so it is
# attached as a pointer from the class to another profile class instead.
#
# That pointer is indistinguishable, as RDF, from a pointer that merely
# RELATES the class to another type (a "leads to", a "related artifact"):
# both are annotation properties whose object is a profile class. A consumer
# following the first must not follow the second, and no rule stated on the
# graph's shape separates them.
#
# <#profileAspect> states the distinction. A property declared
# rdfs:subPropertyOf <#profileAspect> points at a profile class whose
# class-level context the SUBJECT INHERITS, as if it were a parent. A consumer
# extends its ancestor walk along any such property and along nothing else.
# The assertion is a plain triple, matched with no reasoner, in keeping with
# the rest of the pattern.
#
# =============================================================================

<#profileAspect> rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:label "profile aspect" ;
    rdfs:comment "Super-property for a class-level pointer from one profile class to another whose class-level context the subject inherits, as if it were a parent, without the target being a parent in rdfs:subClassOf. A property declared rdfs:subPropertyOf profileAspect is followed by consumers assembling an element's inherited profile context; a property not so declared, however similar in shape, relates the class to another type and is not followed. The object must be a profile class (one carrying profileSpecialization). Takes no part in OWL reasoning or the derivation rules." .
